  "worked_solution": "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-16x+18y & =119 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-16x)+(9{y}^{2}+18y) & =119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-16x)+(9{y}^{2}+18y) & =119 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-4x)+9({y}^{2}+2y) & =119 \\\\ 4[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y+1)^{2}-1] & =119 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}-16+9(y+1)^{2}-9 & =119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{2}-16+9(y+1)^{2}-9 & =119 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+9(y+1)^{2} & =119+16+9 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+9(y+1)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis  $=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the length of the minor axis  $=2\\cdot 4=8.$",
